A||||1||PaUL||My Clutch Nightmare
Z||000000||PaUL||07-07-99||02:54 AM||||Hi, I havent checked the forum for awhile because Ive been busy for the last 5 days fixing my clutch. Last Thursday, my clutch suddenly failed, the car wouldnt go into any gear. At first I thought the problem was inside and I would have to split the trans and engine. So I went about removing the axles and so forth when I discovered that the bolt that holds the clutch release are to the throwout shaft was really worn causing the arm to slip more than 20 degrees. I thought that this must be the problem so I put everything back together but it still wouldnt go. I removed the slave and inserted a screw to push the release are as far as it would go. When it still wouldnt release, I realized it must be internal. So off again with the axles, by this time I was really sick of this. When I unbolted the pressure plate, I discovered that one of the center springs in the clutch disc had come loose. I saw that the clearance between the center part of the flywheel to the hightest point of the clutch disc, the springs was really tight. The springs were rubbing slightly against the flywheel, eventually causing one to come loose. The spring waa caught under the diaphragm. Some of the fingers were bent so I replaced both pressure plate and disc. 
Z||000001||Reckard||07-07-99||05:44 AM||||I had the same problem with my 88Jeep.  One of the springs in the pressure plate came loose and fell between the pressure plate and clutch disk.  The clutch would not release and luckily I was pulling in my driveway.
